    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "ok thanks. i checked the capacitor and it looks a bit swollen like you said. but why does it hum like that? it sounds almost like something is stuck inside or just weirdly inconsistent. is it just the part failing or could it be something else messing with the electrical flow?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The hum is the motor trying to start but failing—it’s getting power, but the capacitor can’t provide the extra voltage boost needed to spin the fan up. A swollen capacitor is a classic sign of internal failure, and that inconsistent hum is often the motor straining against a weak or shorted capacitor, sometimes even pulsing as the electrical load fluctuates. It’s not usually something else messing with the flow, but a bad contactor or a loose wire connection can also cause a similar stutter, so it’s worth checking those connections while you’re at it. Just remember, that capacitor is like a tiny lightning bolt in a can—discharge it safely with an insulated screwdriver before touching anything, or you’ll get a nasty surprise.",
      "source": "target",
